<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · site</p><h1 class="enc-title">Valley of the Kings</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1539–1075 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century-bce">16th century BCE</a> · Also known as Valley of the Gates of the Kings</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Valley of the Kings is a wadi on the west bank of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nile">Nile</a>, opposite <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thebes">Thebes</a> (modern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/luxor">Luxor</a>), where rock-cut tombs were excavated for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pharaoh">pharaohs</a> and powerful nobles of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-kingdom-of-egypt">New Kingdom</a> for nearly 500 years, from the Eighteenth to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/twentieth-dynasty-of-egypt">Twentieth Dynasty</a>. It holds 65 known tombs and chambers, from the simple pit KV54 to KV5, with over 120 chambers for the sons of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ramesses-ii">Ramesses II</a>; only about twenty contain the remains of kings. Almost all were robbed in antiquity, and during the civil war under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ramesses-xi">Ramesses XI</a> the mummies were gathered into caches. The discovery of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tomb-of-tutankhamun">tomb of Tutankhamun</a> in 1922 drew wide attention to the valley, which became a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco-world-heritage-site">UNESCO World Heritage Site</a> in 1979.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/valley-of-the-kings.jpg" alt="Valley of the Kings" width="330" height="232" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Fotograf/Photographer: Peter J. Bubenik (1995) · CC BY-SA 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Luxor,_Tal_der_Könige_(1995,_860x605).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>The valley lies within the Theban Necropolis and has two main sections: the East Valley, where most of the royal tombs are, and the West Valley, also called the Valley of the Monkeys. The Theban hills are dominated by the peak of al-Qurn, known to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egyptians">Egyptians</a> as ta dehent, the Peak.</p><p>Its pyramid shape probably echoed the pyramids of the Old Kingdom. The site's isolation allowed special tomb police, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/medjay">Medjay</a>, to guard it. Its official ancient name was The Great and Majestic Necropolis of the Millions of Years of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pharaoh">Pharaoh</a> in the West of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thebes">Thebes</a>. The rock is limestone interspersed with marl, and violent flash floods dump debris into the tombs.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>At the start of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eighteenth-dynasty-of-egypt">Eighteenth Dynasty</a> only kings were buried in large tombs in the valley, and non-royal people lay in small chambers near their masters. Tombs of the Eighteenth Dynasty vary in plan, such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hatshepsut">Hatshepsut</a>'s, which winds down more than 200 metres, but a bent axis became usual, as in the tombs of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thutmose-iii">Thutmose III</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thutmose-iv">Thutmose IV</a>.</p><p>Later tombs straightened, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ramesses-iii">Ramesses III</a>'s and Ramesses IX's tombs have a straight axis. The early tombs were decorated with the Amduat, describing the sun god's journey through the twelve hours of the night, and from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/horemheb">Horemheb</a>'s time with the Book of Gates. Seti I's KV17 is usually regarded as the finest tomb, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ramesses-ii">Ramesses II</a> built a massive tomb, KV7.</p><p>By the end of the New Kingdom Egypt was in decline, and the valley was heavily plundered. Papyri from the late <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/twentieth-dynasty-of-egypt">Twentieth Dynasty</a> record trials of tomb robbers, and one describes the robbery of the tomb of Ramesses VI. In the Twenty-first Dynasty the priests of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amun">Amun</a> opened most of the tombs and moved the mummies into a few tombs, later most of them to a single cache near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/deir-el-bahari">Deir el-Bahari</a>. Ancient visitors left more than 2,100 Greek and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> graffiti, the earliest dated to 278 BCE. In Coptic times some tombs served as churches, stables and houses.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon</a>'s expedition drew maps and plans of the known tombs in 1799, and in 1827 Wilkinson painted the KV numbers over the entrances of 21 open tombs. The numbers now run from KV1, of Ramesses VII, to KV64, found in 2011, and 65 tombs and chambers are known. Only eleven have been completely recorded. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tutankhamun">Tutankhamun</a>'s tomb, discovered in 1922, drew wide attention, and in 1979 the valley became a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco-world-heritage-site">UNESCO World Heritage Site</a> with the rest of the Theban Necropolis. Eighteen tombs can be opened, rarely at the same time, and Ay's is the only open tomb in the West Valley.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Valley of the Kings” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The walls of the royal tombs in the <b>Valley of the Kings</b> were covered with books of the underworld, such as the Amduat and the Book of Gates, which map the sun’s night journey.</blockquote><a href="/religion/ancient-egyptian-religion">Ancient Egyptian religion · Texts &amp; Teachers</a></li><li><blockquote>Soon after he took power he commissioned a full-size royal tomb in the <b>Valley of the Kings</b>, probably one of two tombs, WV23 or KV57.</blockquote><a href="/work/tomb-of-tutankhamun">Tomb of Tutankhamun · Origins</a></li><li><blockquote>…Deir el-Medina, the “servants in the Place of Truth”, who cut and decorated the royal tombs in the <b>Valley of the Kings</b>; their village dates from the time of Thutmose I. They lived with their families in a walled village of about 68 houses and worked in two…</blockquote><a href="/societies/ancient-egypt">Social Hierarchy of Ancient Egypt</a></li><li><blockquote>Howard Carter's workers uncovered a step cut into the rock of the <b>Valley of the Kings</b>, leading to the nearly intact tomb of Tutankhamun.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/november-4">4 November in history</a></li></ul></section>
